WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.434
-Our next guest
is a Grammy Award

00:00:02.434 --> 00:00:03.636
winning musician and songwriter

00:00:03.636 --> 00:00:05.504
and frontman
of the band Wilco.

00:00:05.504 --> 00:00:07.374
His book,
"World Within a Song --

00:00:07.374 --> 00:00:09.642
Music That Changed My Life
and Life That Changed My Music"

00:00:09.642 --> 00:00:11.110
is available now.
Please welcome back to the show,

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.379
our friend Jeff Tweedy,
everybody.

00:00:13.379 --> 00:00:16.382
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:16.382 --> 00:00:30.129 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:30.129 --> 00:00:31.731
Welcome back.

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:33.165
-Thanks for having me.
-It's lovely.

00:00:33.165 --> 00:00:36.335
Thank you for doing
the jingle to our podcast.

00:00:36.335 --> 00:00:37.804
-Oh. You're welcome.

00:00:37.804 --> 00:00:38.838
[ Laughter ]

00:00:38.838 --> 00:00:40.606
-I feel like you
maybe forgot you did it.

00:00:40.606 --> 00:00:41.640
-I did.
-Yeah?

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:42.641
Alright, well,
thanks for your honesty.

00:00:42.641 --> 00:00:44.009
-Is it good?
-It's great.

00:00:44.009 --> 00:00:45.511
-Yeah.
-Are you --

00:00:45.511 --> 00:00:48.080
You're obviously not getting
as much feedback on it as I am.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.051
-Oh. No. I like haven't. Yeah.
No, somebody told me.

00:00:52.051 --> 00:00:53.319
No. Nobody told me.
-Okay.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:54.621
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:56.456
I love this book because --

00:00:56.456 --> 00:00:59.124
And you've written others.
But this is a very unique take.

00:00:59.124 --> 00:01:02.661
This is the songs,
individual songs where you write

00:01:02.661 --> 00:01:04.530
just a few pages about
how important they are to you,

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:06.666
but you did not open it

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:09.468
with a very sort of hard
sell to the reader

00:01:09.468 --> 00:01:11.805
as far as how special
an experience this would be.

00:01:11.805 --> 00:01:13.540
Can I read you
the very first paragraph?

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.575
-Sure.
-Jeff Tweedy out of the gate.

00:01:15.575 --> 00:01:18.410
This is very absent
of braggadocio.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:20.513
"I'm going to level with you
right off the bat.

00:01:20.513 --> 00:01:22.048
I don't know what I'm doing,

00:01:22.048 --> 00:01:25.218
and I probably don't have any
business writing another book,

00:01:25.218 --> 00:01:28.154
much less one is conceptually
conceived and philosophical,

00:01:28.154 --> 00:01:29.455
as this one aims to be."

00:01:29.455 --> 00:01:30.957
[ Laughter ]

00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:34.160
So now, why do you want to have
people read that right away?

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:35.328
[ Laughter ]

00:01:38.431 --> 00:01:41.334
-It's -- It's honest for one.
-Yeah, it is.

00:01:41.334 --> 00:01:45.471
-But you know,
I actually read a review.

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:47.773
The only review
I've read of this book

00:01:47.773 --> 00:01:50.743
said that they wish
I wouldn't be so humble.

00:01:50.743 --> 00:01:51.744
-Oh.

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:53.012
-And I sent it
to my family.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:54.580
And I said, "See,
I've always told you

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.850
that my humbleness
was my biggest weakness."

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.286
[ Laughter ]

00:02:00.286 --> 00:02:01.554
-One of the things

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:03.389
you actually show
some humility about

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:06.526
is there are songs you loved
the first time you heard them,

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:08.628
and I think we all have
that relationship with songs,

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:11.064
but you also write
sort of a mea culpa

00:02:11.064 --> 00:02:13.298
about a few songs
that you didn't like originally,

00:02:13.298 --> 00:02:17.003
and then you came to appreciate
how great a song it was.

00:02:17.003 --> 00:02:19.772
"Dancing Queen" by -- by ABBA.
-Right.

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:21.240
Undeniable.
-Undeniable.

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.708
But you denied for a long time.
-But I did deny.

00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:24.276
But I denied it. Yeah.

00:02:24.276 --> 00:02:27.146
-So how did it dawn on you
that you were wrong?

00:02:27.146 --> 00:02:29.282
-Um.

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:32.852
I mean, a lot of things
started falling away

00:02:32.852 --> 00:02:36.155
once I got out of
the early adolescent

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:37.690
and adolescent period.

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:41.994
And you start to realize that
there's a lot of great music,

00:02:41.994 --> 00:02:43.262
and it doesn't all have to fit

00:02:43.262 --> 00:02:46.465
into your identity
and character.

00:02:46.465 --> 00:02:49.769
But, yeah, I was just standing
in the grocery store one day,

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:51.837
and it was playing on
the speaker right above me,

00:02:51.837 --> 00:02:56.509
and I was just like, what is
this message from heaven?

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:59.178
[ Laughter ]

00:02:59.178 --> 00:03:02.615
I mean, it's so glorious.
It's so beautiful.

00:03:02.615 --> 00:03:07.153
Yeah. And I'm really happy
when things like that, like,

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:10.356
reveal themselves
as their true nature.

00:03:10.356 --> 00:03:14.026
And you don't have
the whatever context

00:03:14.026 --> 00:03:16.562
you originally heard it in
with people like threatening

00:03:16.562 --> 00:03:19.031
to beat you up
if you listen to it or,

00:03:19.031 --> 00:03:22.001
you know, the different things
that we put in as barriers

00:03:22.001 --> 00:03:24.336
and they all fall away and you
can just listen to something,

00:03:24.336 --> 00:03:25.771
it's really good for you.

00:03:25.771 --> 00:03:27.540
-I think it's also nice
when you point out in the book,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.608
if there's a song that all
of a sudden now you like,

00:03:29.608 --> 00:03:31.678
when you hear your world
just gets better,

00:03:31.678 --> 00:03:33.112
having songs you don't like

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:35.514
is not a positive influence
on your life.

00:03:35.514 --> 00:03:38.183
It's way better to find love
in things.

00:03:38.183 --> 00:03:40.652
-Yeah. I mean, isn't that --
I mean, isn't that the goal?

00:03:40.652 --> 00:03:42.822
-That's the goal.
-Learn to love more, everybody.

00:03:42.822 --> 00:03:43.856
-Yeah.
-Come on.

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:45.091
-"Dancing Queen"
is a real...

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:46.459
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:46.459 --> 00:03:47.826
"Dancing Queen"...

00:03:47.826 --> 00:03:49.395
All right.

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:50.696
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:50.696 --> 00:03:51.998
-I'm running for office.

00:03:51.998 --> 00:03:54.833
-Yeah, yeah, so take
that weird pro-love stance.

00:03:54.833 --> 00:03:56.235
-Yeah.
-Let's see how it goes.

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:57.570
I think it's going to come back
to bite you.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.905
It's like --

00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:02.508
Yeah. Just floated it
out there like a test balloon.

00:04:02.508 --> 00:04:04.710
-Yeah. See how it polls.
-Yeah.

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:07.013
-You talk about coming
to New York

00:04:07.013 --> 00:04:08.514
when you were young
for the first time

00:04:08.514 --> 00:04:11.517
and seeing a street musicians

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:13.886
and trying to bring back
their vibe,

00:04:13.886 --> 00:04:15.187
maybe a little bit
of their fashion

00:04:15.187 --> 00:04:17.022
when you went back
to to your Illinois,

00:04:17.022 --> 00:04:18.824
your small Illinois town?

00:04:18.824 --> 00:04:20.759
-Well,
I was mostly impressed by --

00:04:20.759 --> 00:04:23.996
Well, first of all,
all of the musicians

00:04:23.996 --> 00:04:26.131
I would see on the street
were a million times better

00:04:26.131 --> 00:04:27.333
than -- than I am now.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:28.734
-Right?

00:04:28.734 --> 00:04:30.769
-I was really young and just
learning how to play the guitar,

00:04:30.769 --> 00:04:32.605
and I was like,
wow, I really suck

00:04:32.605 --> 00:04:34.006
and everybody's really great.

00:04:34.006 --> 00:04:35.941
What are they doing
in Carnegie Hall?

00:04:35.941 --> 00:04:39.778
You know? But it wasn't
so much their fashion.

00:04:39.778 --> 00:04:43.249
It was just that they had
liberated themselves to be,

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:46.919
you know, artists
and play music for people

00:04:46.919 --> 00:04:49.922
and felt like confident,
and I wanted some of that.

00:04:49.922 --> 00:04:51.924
And so my mom
bought me a beret.

00:04:51.924 --> 00:04:53.692
-Wow.
-Yeah.

00:04:53.692 --> 00:04:55.828
-And now, were you
the first kid at your school

00:04:55.828 --> 00:04:58.798
in Illinois
to have a beret?

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:02.602
-Not. Not that anybody
in my school knows.

00:05:02.602 --> 00:05:03.769
-Well, did you not
wear it to school?

00:05:03.769 --> 00:05:05.672
-No, no, I made it home
with the beret.

00:05:05.672 --> 00:05:09.008
-Okay.
-And I got out of my mom's car

00:05:09.008 --> 00:05:10.676
with the beret on.
-To go to school.

00:05:10.676 --> 00:05:11.944
-To go to school.
-Yeah.

00:05:11.944 --> 00:05:14.981
Yeah. And quickly...

00:05:14.981 --> 00:05:16.849
[ Laughter ]

00:05:16.849 --> 00:05:18.518
-Did it just dawn on you
that it wasn't going to work?

00:05:18.518 --> 00:05:20.619
-I locked eyes with somebody

00:05:20.619 --> 00:05:24.123
and realized the colossal
mistake I was making.

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:25.457
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:25.457 --> 00:05:27.826
-That I was never going
to be able to pull this off.

00:05:27.826 --> 00:05:29.762
-And even now,
you don't beret it.

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:31.530
-No, I mean, I'm wearing a --
Yeah.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:33.266
-Yeah.
-No, I think that there's Bel--

00:05:33.266 --> 00:05:35.334
-Nobody thinks
you're not pulling this off.

00:05:35.334 --> 00:05:37.136
-Oh, no.
-Yeah.

00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:40.238
-Anybody can pull this off.

00:05:40.238 --> 00:05:42.541
No, there's Belleville-Jeff,
and there's beret-Jeff.

00:05:42.541 --> 00:05:45.544
And -- And the Belleville's
where I grew up.

00:05:45.544 --> 00:05:48.047
And they're in a constant,
you know.

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:49.649
[ Laughter ]

00:05:49.649 --> 00:05:52.818
Even to this day on stage
right now, at this very moment.

00:05:52.818 --> 00:05:53.852
-Yeah.
-One of them is like,

00:05:53.852 --> 00:05:55.587
"Who do you think you are?"
-Wow.

00:05:55.587 --> 00:05:57.957
-So you're telling me
that Beret-Jeff is inside you,

00:05:57.957 --> 00:06:00.159
right now, wondering when
he's going to get his time.

00:06:00.159 --> 00:06:02.295
-Yeah, yeah.
Once you start, like,

00:06:02.295 --> 00:06:03.763
talking about
how great the book is.

00:06:03.763 --> 00:06:04.896
[ Laughter ]

00:06:04.896 --> 00:06:06.165
-Yeah. Here's the first
paragraph

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:07.566
in beret-Jeff's
version is like,

00:06:07.566 --> 00:06:09.501
"You're about to have
your mind [bleep] blown."

00:06:09.501 --> 00:06:12.538
-Exactly, exactly.
Yeah.

00:06:12.538 --> 00:06:15.541
This -- This book should
revolutionize literature.

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:16.875
[ Laughter ]

00:06:16.875 --> 00:06:20.312
-Now you talk about
and Mavis Staples, for example,

00:06:20.312 --> 00:06:22.247
is someone you got to talk to

00:06:22.247 --> 00:06:25.251
about how much one of her songs
meant to you.

00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:28.854
I would imagine you have people
who come up to you all the time

00:06:28.854 --> 00:06:34.193
to tell you a story about
one of your songs meant to them.

00:06:34.193 --> 00:06:36.762
-Yeah, like the theme song
for your podcast?

00:06:36.762 --> 00:06:38.030
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:39.464
And let me just say
I got everything I needed

00:06:39.464 --> 00:06:40.466
from you out of that.

00:06:40.466 --> 00:06:42.267
-Yeah.
-That was the dream.

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:43.936
-That's an example
of the only one

00:06:43.936 --> 00:06:46.071
that no one has come up to me
and talked to me about.

00:06:46.071 --> 00:06:47.740
-Alright.
-Yeah.

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.141
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.141 --> 00:06:50.175
No, it's just...

00:06:50.175 --> 00:06:51.711
-Is it a weird
message to receive?

00:06:51.711 --> 00:06:53.478
-Oh, it's such a --
Yeah, it's a really --

00:06:53.478 --> 00:06:56.982
it's a -- it's such a gift to,
to make something and,

00:06:56.982 --> 00:07:01.053
and then come to understand
that it really did find somebody

00:07:01.053 --> 00:07:02.922
and mean something to somebody.

00:07:02.922 --> 00:07:05.591
And I'm going to cry
now talking about it. No.

00:07:05.591 --> 00:07:08.627
But it's really it's
a seriously beautiful thing

00:07:08.627 --> 00:07:10.029
to get to be a part of.

00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:14.133
But you also can't look at it
directly very long

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:18.304
because it's not really
what got you to where you are.

00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:21.740
It's not. I didn't really think
that that would happen

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.242
when I started writing songs,

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:26.445
and I can't think about it
when I'm writing songs now,

00:07:26.445 --> 00:07:29.115
because that's just too much
weight to put on it, but.

00:07:29.115 --> 00:07:30.716
-Yeah,
but it still must be nice.

00:07:30.716 --> 00:07:32.485
I mean, again, right? You can't
think about it at the time,

00:07:32.485 --> 00:07:35.253
but it must be just such
a wonderful sort of annuity

00:07:35.253 --> 00:07:36.655
to your work that someday

00:07:36.655 --> 00:07:38.558
somebody might come up to you
on any given song

00:07:38.558 --> 00:07:40.159
and just be like,
hey, this meant this to me.

00:07:40.159 --> 00:07:41.660
-Oh, it's the best.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:43.329
I mean, it's -- I always say,
that's what it's for.

00:07:43.329 --> 00:07:46.198
I mean, it is kind of like
the highest aspiration

00:07:46.198 --> 00:07:47.967
is that you could,
you know,

00:07:47.967 --> 00:07:51.603
keep somebody company in a,
in a, in a moment

00:07:51.603 --> 00:07:54.340
where they need some,
some consolation, you know.

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:57.209
And then music does that
for all of us, I think.

00:07:57.209 --> 00:07:58.844
-Well, it's a wonderful book.

00:07:58.844 --> 00:08:02.381
The last Wilco album
came out September, also great.

00:08:02.381 --> 00:08:03.782
-Thank you so much.

00:08:03.782 --> 00:08:05.083
-And it is just
lovely to see you.

00:08:05.083 --> 00:08:06.218
I'm going to see you later
on tonight as well.

00:08:06.218 --> 00:08:07.786
-That's right.
-Double date.

00:08:07.786 --> 00:08:08.855
Jeff Tweedy, everybody.

00:08:08.855 --> 00:08:11.490
"World Within a Song"
is available now.

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:12.859
We'll be right back
with Ms. Pat.

